1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,565
This is ECE 358, video 23. The topic is link-layer addressing, ARP, and Ethernet. This is Chapter 6, slides 44 to 64.

2
00:00:13,015 --> 00:00:18,519
After this video, you can explain MAC addresses, and why an interface needs one.

3
00:00:18,969 --> 00:00:22,852
You can show the ARP query and reply, and the ARP table.

4
00:00:23,302 --> 00:00:27,910
You can fill in the MAC and IP addresses of a frame on each hop.

5
00:00:28,360 --> 00:00:33,715
You can explain every field of the Ethernet frame, and bus versus switched Ethernet.

6
00:00:34,165 --> 00:00:39,711
And you can answer the question bank, Link Layer questions 45 to 55.

7
00:00:40,161 --> 00:00:45,580
This laptop has one interface, its network interface card, or NIC.

8
00:00:46,030 --> 00:00:53,283
Recall the IP address from Chapter 4. It is a 32-bit network-layer address of the interface.

9
00:00:53,733 --> 00:00:59,131
The IP address forwards a datagram from host to host, across many networks.

10
00:00:59,581 --> 00:01:05,170
The interface also has a MAC address. It is a 48-bit link-layer address.

11
00:01:05,620 --> 00:01:13,535
Its job is local. It gets a frame from one interface to another physically-connected interface, in the same subnet.

12
00:01:13,985 --> 00:01:18,379
Other names are LAN address, physical address, and Ethernet address.

13
00:01:18,829 --> 00:01:29,987
We write a MAC address in hexadecimal, as 6 pairs of digits. The example on the slide is 1A, 2F, BB, 76, 0 9, AD.

14
00:01:30,437 --> 00:01:38,351
Each hex digit is 4 bits. For example, 1 is 0 0 0 1, and A is 1 0 1 0.

15
00:01:38,801 --> 00:01:46,929
There are 12 hex digits. 12 times 4 bits gives 48 bits. 48 divided by 8 gives 6 bytes.

16
00:01:47,379 --> 00:01:53,417
The MAC address is burned into the ROM of the NIC. Sometimes, software can set it.

17
00:01:53,867 --> 00:02:00,267
Here is one LAN, wired or wireless. Four interfaces, A, B, C and D, connect to it.

18
00:02:00,717 --> 00:02:05,495
Each interface has a unique 48-bit MAC address, shown in yellow.

19
00:02:05,945 --> 00:02:09,913
Each interface also has an IP address, shown in purple.

20
00:02:10,363 --> 00:02:21,713
All four IP addresses start with the same subnet part: 137 dot 196 dot 7. The subnet is slash 24.

21
00:02:22,163 --> 00:02:29,053
Now compare the MAC addresses. They have no common part. The MAC address does not depend on the subnet.

22
00:02:29,503 --> 00:02:36,053
Who gives out MAC addresses? The IEEE administers the 48-bit MAC address space.

23
00:02:36,503 --> 00:02:42,177
A manufacturer buys a portion of the space. This makes every MAC address unique.

24
00:02:42,627 --> 00:02:49,497
The slide gives an analogy. A MAC address is like a Social Security Number. It stays the same.

25
00:02:49,947 --> 00:02:55,152
An IP address is like a postal address. It changes when the host moves.

26
00:02:55,602 --> 00:03:02,023
The MAC address space is flat. A MAC address has no structure that tells where the interface is.

27
00:03:02,473 --> 00:03:17,407
Here the interface is on LAN 1. Its IP address, 137 dot 196 dot 7 dot 23, belongs to subnet 137 dot 196 dot 7.

28
00:03:17,857 --> 00:03:23,937
Move the interface to LAN 2. It keeps its MAC address. So the MAC address is portable.

29
00:03:24,387 --> 00:03:28,589
But the IP address must change, because it depends on the subnet.

30
00:03:29,039 --> 00:03:34,394
Here is why an interface needs a MAC address. A sends a frame to B on the LAN.

31
00:03:34,844 --> 00:03:39,772
On a shared LAN, every interface receives the frame: B, C and D.

32
00:03:40,222 --> 00:03:48,094
Each adapter compares the destination MAC address of the frame with its own address. The NIC hardware does this.

33
00:03:48,544 --> 00:03:58,293
B's address, 58 23 D7 FA 20 B0, matches. So B's adapter passes the data up to the network layer.

34
00:03:58,743 --> 00:04:04,141
The addresses of C and D do not match. So their adapters discard the frame.

35
00:04:04,591 --> 00:04:15,300
So, before A can send, A must know the MAC address of the destination interface. A knows only the IP address. The next protocol solves this problem.

36
00:04:15,750 --> 00:04:30,278
A knows the IP address of the destination interface. A must find its MAC address. For example, A knows its first-hop router's IP address from DHCP, and needs the router's MAC address.

37
00:04:30,728 --> 00:04:37,747
The answer is ARP, the address resolution protocol. ARP maps an IP address to a MAC address.

38
00:04:38,197 --> 00:04:43,615
Each IP node on the LAN, host or router, runs ARP and has an ARP table.

39
00:04:44,065 --> 00:04:55,244
Each entry of the ARP table is a triple. The first part is an IP address, here 137 dot 196 dot 7 dot 14.

40
00:04:55,694 --> 00:04:59,427
The second part is the MAC address of that IP address.

41
00:04:59,877 --> 00:05:03,099
The third part is the TTL, time to live.

42
00:05:03,549 --> 00:05:08,861
After the TTL, the node forgets the mapping. Typically this is 20 minutes.

43
00:05:09,311 --> 00:05:19,401
A wants to send a datagram to B. A knows B's IP address, 137 dot 196 dot 7 dot 14.

44
00:05:19,851 --> 00:05:24,950
But B's MAC address is not in A's ARP table. The table is empty.

45
00:05:25,400 --> 00:05:32,760
Step 1. A broadcasts an ARP query. The query holds B's IP address as the target IP.

46
00:05:33,210 --> 00:05:38,202
The frame's destination MAC address is the broadcast address, all F's.

47
00:05:38,652 --> 00:05:44,049
The query also holds A's own MAC address and IP address, as the source.

48
00:05:44,499 --> 00:05:52,307
All nodes on the LAN receive the query. C and D do not reply, because the target IP is not theirs.

49
00:05:52,757 --> 00:05:55,083
B sees its own IP address.

50
00:05:55,533 --> 00:06:06,114
Step 2. B replies to A with an ARP response. B sends it only to A, not as a broadcast. So the destination MAC is A's MAC address.

51
00:06:06,564 --> 00:06:14,287
The response gives B's MAC address, 58 23 D7 FA 20 B0, as the target MAC.

52
00:06:14,737 --> 00:06:24,230
Step 3. A receives B's reply. A adds an entry for B to its ARP table: B's IP address and B's MAC address.

53
00:06:24,680 --> 00:06:31,293
The TTL on the slide is 500. Now A can send the datagram to B's MAC address.

54
00:06:31,743 --> 00:06:35,711
Now A sends a datagram to B. B is on another subnet.

55
00:06:36,161 --> 00:06:38,359
The datagram goes through router R.

56
00:06:38,809 --> 00:06:42,243
R has two interfaces, one on each subnet.

57
00:06:42,693 --> 00:06:46,427
So R has two IP addresses and two MAC addresses.

58
00:06:46,877 --> 00:06:51,122
We assume three facts. First, A knows B's IP address.

59
00:06:51,572 --> 00:06:57,247
Second, A knows the IP address of its first-hop router R, from DHCP.

60
00:06:57,697 --> 00:07:00,726
Third, A knows R's MAC address, from ARP.

61
00:07:01,176 --> 00:07:12,739
Step 1. A creates an IP datagram. The source IP is A, 111 dot 111 dot 111 dot 111.

62
00:07:13,189 --> 00:07:22,255
The destination IP is B, 222 dot 222 dot 222 dot 222.

63
00:07:22,705 --> 00:07:37,788
Step 2. A puts the datagram in a link-layer frame. The frame's destination MAC address is R's MAC address, E6 E9 00 17 BB 4B. It is not B's MAC address.

64
00:07:38,238 --> 00:07:45,705
The source MAC address is A's MAC address, 74 29 9C E8 FF 55.

65
00:07:46,155 --> 00:07:51,936
Step 3. A sends the frame on subnet 1. R's left interface receives it.

66
00:07:52,386 --> 00:07:58,573
Step 4. R removes the datagram from the frame and passes it up to the IP layer.

67
00:07:59,023 --> 00:08:09,305
Step 1. R determines the outgoing interface. B is on subnet 222 dot 222 dot 222.

68
00:08:09,755 --> 00:08:23,558
So the datagram leaves through R's right interface, 222 dot 222 dot 222 dot 220. R passes the datagram to the link layer.

69
00:08:24,008 --> 00:08:35,464
Step 2. R creates a new link-layer frame. The source MAC address is R's right interface, 1A 23 F9 CD 06 9B.

70
00:08:35,914 --> 00:08:46,559
The destination MAC address is B's MAC address, 49 BD D2 C7 56 2A. R gets it with ARP on subnet 2.

71
00:08:47,009 --> 00:08:51,148
The IP source and the IP destination do not change.

72
00:08:51,598 --> 00:08:55,225
Step 3. R transmits the frame on subnet 2.

73
00:08:55,675 --> 00:09:01,712
Step 4. B receives the frame, extracts the datagram, and passes it up to IP.

74
00:09:02,162 --> 00:09:08,690
Compare the two frames. The MAC source is A on hop 1, and R's right interface on hop 2.

75
00:09:09,140 --> 00:09:14,473
The MAC destination is R's left interface on hop 1, and B on hop 2.

76
00:09:14,923 --> 00:09:19,211
So the MAC source and the MAC destination change on each hop.

77
00:09:19,661 --> 00:09:22,200
The IP source is A on both hops.

78
00:09:22,650 --> 00:09:25,871
The IP destination is B on both hops.

79
00:09:26,321 --> 00:09:29,756
So the IP addresses stay the same from end to end.

80
00:09:30,206 --> 00:09:37,011
A MAC address works on one link. An IP address works from the source host to the destination host.

81
00:09:37,461 --> 00:09:43,264
For a destination on another subnet, the frame goes to the first-hop router's MAC address.

82
00:09:43,714 --> 00:09:50,839
Ethernet is the dominant wired LAN technology. It was the first widely used LAN technology.

83
00:09:51,289 --> 00:09:53,039
It is simpler and cheap.

84
00:09:53,489 --> 00:09:56,113
One chip can support multiple speeds.

85
00:09:56,563 --> 00:10:02,088
Ethernet speeds went from 10 megabits per second to 400 gigabits per second.

86
00:10:02,538 --> 00:10:13,567
The axis uses a log scale. Most steps to the right are 10 times faster: 10 to 100 megabits, 100 megabits to 1 gigabit, and so on.

87
00:10:14,017 --> 00:10:21,996
One step is only 4 times faster: 10 to 40 gigabits per second. So that step is shorter on the axis.

88
00:10:22,446 --> 00:10:33,923
Ethernet has two physical topologies. The first is the bus. It was popular until the mid 1990s. All nodes connect to one coaxial cable.

89
00:10:34,373 --> 00:10:38,384
If A and D transmit at the same time, their frames collide.

90
00:10:38,834 --> 00:10:42,418
On a bus, all nodes are in the same collision domain.

91
00:10:42,868 --> 00:10:47,711
A collision domain is a set of interfaces whose frames can collide.

92
00:10:48,161 --> 00:10:53,281
The second topology is switched. An active link-layer switch is in the center.

93
00:10:53,731 --> 00:10:57,507
Each spoke runs its own, separate Ethernet protocol.

94
00:10:57,957 --> 00:11:05,701
So A can send to A prime, and B can send to B prime, at the same time. The nodes do not collide with each other.

95
00:11:06,151 --> 00:11:13,724
A hub looked like a switch, but it behaved like a bus. So all nodes on a hub are in one collision domain.

96
00:11:14,174 --> 00:11:23,966
The sending interface encapsulates the IP datagram in an Ethernet frame. The datagram goes in the data field, also called the payload.

97
00:11:24,416 --> 00:11:27,872
The frame starts with the preamble. It is 8 bytes.

98
00:11:28,322 --> 00:11:48,290
The preamble synchronizes the clock rates of the receiver and the sender. It is 7 bytes of 1 0 1 0 1 0 1 0, then one byte of 1 0 1 0 1 0 1 1. 7 times 8 bits, plus 8 bits, is 64 bits, or 8 bytes.

99
00:11:48,740 --> 00:11:52,260
The last two ones tell the receiver that the frame starts.

100
00:11:52,710 --> 00:11:56,806
Next comes the destination MAC address. It is 6 bytes.

101
00:11:57,256 --> 00:12:00,328
Then the source MAC address, also 6 bytes.

102
00:12:00,778 --> 00:12:08,181
The type field is 2 bytes. It tells which higher-layer protocol is in the payload. Mostly it is IP.

103
00:12:08,631 --> 00:12:12,385
The receiver uses the type field to demultiplex up.

104
00:12:12,835 --> 00:12:19,619
The frame ends with the CRC, 4 bytes. The receiver does a cyclic redundancy check.

105
00:12:20,069 --> 00:12:27,536
If it detects an error, it drops the frame. Video 21 showed how the CRC is calculated.

106
00:12:27,986 --> 00:12:32,103
Here is what the receiving adapter does with a frame that arrives.

107
00:12:32,553 --> 00:12:39,167
First it checks the destination address: is it the adapter's own address, or the broadcast address?

108
00:12:39,617 --> 00:12:42,305
If not, the adapter discards the frame.

109
00:12:42,755 --> 00:12:50,243
If the address matches, or it is a broadcast, for example an ARP query, the adapter checks the CRC.

110
00:12:50,693 --> 00:12:59,034
If the frame is good, the adapter passes the data to the network-layer protocol. The type field selects that protocol.

111
00:12:59,484 --> 00:13:03,409
If the CRC detects an error, the frame is dropped.

112
00:13:03,859 --> 00:13:09,086
In no case does the receiver send an acknowledgement, positive or negative.

113
00:13:09,536 --> 00:13:16,939
Ethernet is connectionless. There is no handshake between the sending NIC and the receiving NIC.

114
00:13:17,389 --> 00:13:22,978
Ethernet is unreliable. Here frame 2 has a CRC error and is dropped.

115
00:13:23,428 --> 00:13:31,385
The receiving NIC does not send ACKs or NAKs to the sending NIC. So the sender never learns about the loss.

116
00:13:31,835 --> 00:13:44,507
The data of a dropped frame is recovered only if the sending host uses a higher-layer reliable data transfer protocol, for example TCP. Otherwise, the data is lost.

117
00:13:44,957 --> 00:13:52,936
Ethernet's MAC protocol is CSMA/CD with binary exponential backoff, from video 22.

118
00:13:53,386 --> 00:14:06,549
But switch ports are full-duplex by default, so CSMA/CD does not run there. If a switch port is configured as half-duplex, the host runs CSMA/CD.

119
00:14:06,999 --> 00:14:16,193
There are many different Ethernet standards, called 802.3. They all use a common MAC protocol and the same frame format.

120
00:14:16,643 --> 00:14:24,473
They have different speeds: 2, 10, and 100 megabits per second, and 1, 10, and 40 gigabits per second.

121
00:14:24,923 --> 00:14:30,747
They also use different physical-layer media. Some use copper, that is, twisted pair.

122
00:14:31,197 --> 00:14:32,647
Others use fiber.

123
00:14:33,097 --> 00:14:37,833
The names, such as 100 BASE T X, name the speed and the medium.

124
00:14:38,283 --> 00:14:42,486
So the link layer stays the same, and the physical layer changes.

125
00:14:42,936 --> 00:14:53,005
Question 45 asks for the other names of a MAC address. Question 46 asks how MAC addresses are used. Pause the video and try it.

126
00:14:53,455 --> 00:15:00,047
Answer to 45. From slide 45, a MAC address is also called a LAN address.

127
00:15:00,497 --> 00:15:02,844
It is also called a physical address.

128
00:15:03,294 --> 00:15:05,897
And it is also called an Ethernet address.

129
00:15:06,347 --> 00:15:16,651
Answer to 46. A MAC address is used locally. It gets a frame from one interface to another physically-connected interface in the same subnet.

130
00:15:17,101 --> 00:15:21,005
The sender puts the destination MAC address in the frame.

131
00:15:21,455 --> 00:15:26,703
The destination adapter passes the frame up. All other adapters discard it.

132
00:15:27,153 --> 00:15:42,726
Question 47. Every host and router interface has an IP address. Why does it also need a MAC address? Question 48. How many bits is a MAC address, and what is the broadcast pattern? Pause the video and try it.

133
00:15:43,176 --> 00:15:51,987
Answer to 47. The IP address is for forwarding a datagram from host to host. It stays A to B on every hop.

134
00:15:52,437 --> 00:15:57,834
But on each link, the frame must name the next interface. The MAC address does this.

135
00:15:58,284 --> 00:16:03,660
The adapters filter frames in hardware, so other hosts do not process the frame.

136
00:16:04,110 --> 00:16:09,913
Also, the MAC address is fixed and portable, while the IP address changes with the subnet.

137
00:16:10,363 --> 00:16:17,104
Answer to 48. A MAC address is 6 bytes. 6 times 8 bits gives 48 bits.

138
00:16:17,554 --> 00:16:21,245
The broadcast address has all 48 bits set to 1.

139
00:16:21,695 --> 00:16:27,796
Each byte of eight ones is F F in hexadecimal. So the broadcast address is all F's.

140
00:16:28,246 --> 00:16:41,494
Question 49. What does flat mean for the MAC address space? Question 50. A subnet has 20 interfaces. Is there a constraint among their MAC addresses? Pause the video and try it.

141
00:16:41,944 --> 00:16:51,245
Answer to 49. Flat means a MAC address has no structure that depends on location. Here are four interfaces of one subnet.

142
00:16:51,695 --> 00:16:55,407
Their MAC addresses have no relationship with each other.

143
00:16:55,857 --> 00:17:04,540
In contrast, all their IP addresses share the subnet part, 137 dot 196 dot 7.

144
00:17:04,990 --> 00:17:10,665
Because the space is flat, an interface can move to another LAN and keep its MAC address.

145
00:17:11,115 --> 00:17:19,413
Answer to 50. The only constraint is that the 20 MAC addresses are all different. They do not need a common prefix.

146
00:17:19,863 --> 00:17:32,663
Question 51. Which protocol does a host run to learn the MAC address of its first-hop router? Question 52. What is stored in an ARP table? Pause the video and try it.

147
00:17:33,113 --> 00:17:45,679
Answer to 51. The host runs ARP, the address resolution protocol. It knows the router's IP address from DHCP, and it asks for the matching MAC address.

148
00:17:46,129 --> 00:17:49,243
The ARP reply gives the router's MAC address.

149
00:17:49,693 --> 00:17:58,525
Answer to 52. Each entry of the ARP table is a triple: an IP address, the matching MAC address, and a TTL.

150
00:17:58,975 --> 00:18:04,607
The TTL is the time after which the mapping is forgotten, typically 20 minutes.

151
00:18:05,057 --> 00:18:18,156
Question 53. Describe ARP as a message sequence chart. Show the message types and their contents. We build it from slides 49 to 51. Pause the video and try it.

152
00:18:18,606 --> 00:18:23,705
Message 1 is the ARP query. A broadcasts it to all nodes on the LAN.

153
00:18:24,155 --> 00:18:28,336
Because it is a broadcast, the destination MAC is all F's.

154
00:18:28,786 --> 00:18:32,327
The query holds A's MAC address as the source MAC.

155
00:18:32,777 --> 00:18:36,063
It holds A's IP address as the source IP.

156
00:18:36,513 --> 00:18:41,035
And it holds the target IP address, which is B's IP address.

157
00:18:41,485 --> 00:18:46,243
C and D do not reply, because the target IP is not theirs.

158
00:18:46,693 --> 00:18:54,330
Message 2 is the ARP reply. B sends it only to A, so the destination MAC is A's MAC address.

159
00:18:54,780 --> 00:18:56,999
The source MAC is B's MAC address.

160
00:18:57,449 --> 00:19:04,915
The reply holds the target IP address and the target MAC address. The target MAC is B's MAC address.

161
00:19:05,365 --> 00:19:09,589
Then A adds B's entry to its ARP table, with a TTL.

162
00:19:10,039 --> 00:19:13,325
Now A sends the data frame to B's MAC address.

163
00:19:13,775 --> 00:19:25,636
Question 54. Compare the bus topology with switched Ethernet. Let there be 10 interfaces, and let all switch ports be full-duplex. Pause the video and try it.

164
00:19:26,086 --> 00:19:33,361
Topology. A bus is linear: one cable. A switch is a star: each host has its own link to the switch.

165
00:19:33,811 --> 00:19:39,741
Collision. On a bus, frames can collide. With a full-duplex switch, there are no collisions.

166
00:19:40,191 --> 00:19:46,442
Number of collision domains. On a bus, all 10 interfaces are in one collision domain.

167
00:19:46,892 --> 00:19:54,380
With a switch, each port is its own collision domain. There are 10 ports, so there are 10 collision domains.

168
00:19:54,830 --> 00:20:02,446
Can A to A prime and B to B prime succeed at the same time? On a bus, no. They collide. With a switch, yes.

169
00:20:02,896 --> 00:20:12,261
Hierarchical organization. A bus has none. Switches can connect to other switches in a hierarchy, as on slide 73.

170
00:20:12,711 --> 00:20:19,005
Store-and-forward. A bus only carries the signal. A switch stores and forwards frames.

171
00:20:19,455 --> 00:20:27,711
Question 55. Explain each field of the Ethernet frame: its length and its purpose. Pause the video and try it.

172
00:20:28,161 --> 00:20:43,457
The preamble is 8 bytes: 7 bytes of 1 0 1 0 1 0 1 0, then 1 byte of 1 0 1 0 1 0 1 1. It synchronizes the clock rates of the receiver and the sender.

173
00:20:43,907 --> 00:20:53,912
The destination MAC address is 6 bytes. The adapter keeps the frame only if the destination is its own address or the broadcast address.

174
00:20:54,362 --> 00:21:00,143
The source MAC address is also 6 bytes. It is the address of the sending interface.

175
00:21:00,593 --> 00:21:09,127
The type field is 2 bytes. It names the higher-layer protocol, mostly IP. The receiver uses it to demultiplex.

176
00:21:09,577 --> 00:21:22,611
The payload holds the IP datagram. The Chapter 6 slides do not give its length. Chapter 4 says an Ethernet MTU is typically 1500 bytes or less.

177
00:21:23,061 --> 00:21:31,211
The CRC is 4 bytes. The receiver uses it to detect errors. If it finds an error, it drops the frame.

178
00:21:31,661 --> 00:21:57,922
One more practice question, built on the slide 52 network. A sends one datagram to 111 dot 111 dot 111 dot 112, and one to 222 dot 222 dot 222 dot 221. What are the MAC destination and IP destination of each frame that A sends? Pause the video and try it.

179
00:21:58,372 --> 00:22:15,673
Compare the subnet parts. 111 dot 111 dot 111 dot 112 starts with 111 dot 111 dot 111, the same as A. So it is on A's own subnet.

180
00:22:16,123 --> 00:22:26,171
So A uses ARP to find that interface's MAC address, C C 49 DE D0 AB 7D, and sends the frame to it directly.

181
00:22:26,621 --> 00:22:35,240
The IP destination is 111 dot 111 dot 111 dot 112.

182
00:22:35,690 --> 00:22:52,309
222 dot 222 dot 222 dot 221 starts with 222 dot 222 dot 222. This is not A's subnet. So it is on another subnet.

183
00:22:52,759 --> 00:23:03,361
So the frame goes to the first-hop router. The MAC destination is R's left interface, E6 E9 00 17 BB 4B.

184
00:23:03,811 --> 00:23:13,283
The IP destination is still 222 dot 222 dot 222 dot 221.

185
00:23:13,733 --> 00:23:26,171
To recap. A MAC address is a 48-bit link-layer address. It is used locally, on one link. It is flat, unique, and portable. The broadcast address is all F's.

186
00:23:26,621 --> 00:23:32,573
An IP address is a 32-bit network-layer address. It depends on the subnet.

187
00:23:33,023 --> 00:23:46,996
ARP maps an IP address to a MAC address. The query is a broadcast with the target IP. The reply is a unicast with the target MAC. The ARP table holds IP, MAC, and TTL.

188
00:23:47,446 --> 00:23:57,153
To reach another subnet, the frame goes to the first-hop router. The MAC addresses change on each hop. The IP addresses do not change.

189
00:23:57,603 --> 00:24:07,885
The Ethernet frame has a preamble of 8 bytes, two addresses of 6 bytes, a type of 2 bytes, the payload, and a CRC of 4 bytes.

190
00:24:08,335 --> 00:24:19,407
Ethernet is connectionless and unreliable. A bus is one collision domain; a switch has one per port. The next video shows how switches forward frames.
